<DocPage
  title="Getting started · vipyr"
  icon="/brand/vipyr/vipyr-icon.svg"
  description="A first vipyr call, your own .viv file as a Python module, and the tools for feature code: point-in-time and parity checks, and a fitted pipeline as one C99 file."
  tree={vipyrTree(Astro.url.pathname)}
  toc={[['first', 'A first call'], ['own', 'Your own Viv'], ['tools', 'Feature code and models'], ['next', 'Where next']]}>
  <h1>Getting started</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    vipyr does two things: it calls Viv's checked programs as Python functions, and it loads your own
    <code>.viv</code> files as Python modules. Below are both, then the tools for feature pipelines.
  </p>

  <h2 id="first">A first call</h2>
  <p>
    Every page writes <code>vp.scf.run(...)</code> for <code>vipyr.scf.run</code>. From a
    <a href="/vipyr/install">checkout</a>, <code>vp.scf</code> imports directly; on PyPI it is
    planned as <code>pip install "vipyr[chem]"</code>.
  </p>
  <Code lang="python" code={START} />
  <p>
    The first call in a process compiles the program to a C library, or loads the one built last time; later calls
    reuse it. A wrong argument is refused before anything compiles, with the parameter's name in the message.
  </p>

  <h2 id="own">Your own Viv</h2>
  <p>A <code>.viv</code> file of your own, <code>kernels.viv</code>:</p>
  <Code lang="viv" code={VIV} />
  <p>
    <code>vp.load</code> compiles it and returns a module. Each top-level def whose parameters are annotated becomes a
    Python function that takes NumPy arrays, or a pandas or polars DataFrame whose columns go to the parameters by
    name. A compile error is a <code>SyntaxError</code> that points into the <code>.viv</code> file.
  </p>
  <Code lang="python" code={OWN} />
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>%load_ext vipyr</code> in Jupyter adds the <code>%%viv NAME</code> cell magic: a cell of Viv defs becomes
      the module <code>NAME</code>, and each call is recorded in <code>.viv_runs.jsonl</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      A package of your own builds its wheel with <code>build-backend = "vipyr.build"</code> and lists its
      <code>.viv</code> modules under <code>[tool.vipyr]</code> in <code>pyproject.toml</code>.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>The language itself is introduced in the <a href="/tour">Viv tour</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="tools">Feature code and models</h2>
  <p>
    Three tools for the code between your data and a model, all on your own functions:
  </p>
  <Code lang="python" code={PIT} />
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>vp.check_point_in_time</code> checks a feature function for future leakage: no output row may depend on
      an input row with a later timestamp.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>vp.check_parity</code> checks that serving computes what training computed: it replays the rows one
      request at a time and compares with the batch result bit for bit.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>vp.compile.compile_model</code> (or <code>vipyr compile model.pkl -o model.c</code>) writes a fitted
      scikit-learn pipeline and your custom features as one C99 file, with a self-test that runs on the target
      machine.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Per-entity features with state, such as sessions, rolling counts and time since the last event, are in
    <a href="/vipyr/features"><code>vp.features</code></a>.
  </p>

